  "body": "https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/dom.html#element-definitions\r\n\r\n> For example, an element that is both flow content and phrasing content can be used anywhere that either flow content or phrasing content is expected, but since anywhere that flow content is expected, phrasing content is also expected \\(since all phrasing content is flow content\\), only \"where phrasing content is expected\" will be listed\\.\r\n\r\nThe \"all X is Y\" example given here is backwards, or the surrounding text is. If \"all phrasing content is flow content\", then flow content is the superclass; this makes \"anywhere that flow content is expected, phrasing content is also expected\" wrong, and using just \"where phrasing content is expected\" incomplete.",
